  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=24-6{y}^{2}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-3$ and $y=-2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-2}(24-6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,6{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24y\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2}-2{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{-2} \\\\  & =24(-2-(-3))-2(-8-(-27)) \\\\  & =24-38 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-14|=14.$",
